WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.029
An affine layer combines inputs with weights and a bias. Stacking affine layers alone still produces

00:00:07.029 --> 00:00:13.859
one affine map, so extra depth would not create new nonlinear decision regions. An activation introduces

00:00:13.859 --> 00:00:16.420
an input-dependent response between those maps.

00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:21.733
Weights mix or scale input coordinates; the bias shifts the activation boundary.

00:00:22.233 --> 00:00:29.183
ReLU keeps positive preactivations and sets negative ones to zero. The complete map is piecewise linear.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:36.193
What happens to the output sensitivity when the preactivation becomes negative? Pause here and make a

00:00:36.193 --> 00:00:37.410
prediction before continuing.

00:00:38.533 --> 00:00:45.564
At the kink there is no ordinary derivative. Autograd implementations choose a convention, often zero; away

00:00:45.564 --> 00:00:52.594
from it the derivative is zero or one. For our starting inputs, Preactivation: 2. ReLU output:

00:00:52.594 --> 00:00:53.033
2.

00:00:53.533 --> 00:01:01.444
Without a nonlinearity between affine layers, their composition is still one affine map. ReLU introduces input-dependent

00:01:01.444 --> 00:01:02.433
active regions.

00:01:02.933 --> 00:01:10.143
Distinguish the affine preactivation from the post-activation feature. Each has a separate role in the computational

00:01:10.143 --> 00:01:10.593
graph.

